WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.692
If you want a RISC-V processor design that you can study,

2
00:00:04.692 --> 00:00:10.782
modify and integrate into a system, CVA6 offers a concrete starting point.

3
00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:15.145
It is a configurable open-source processor-IP family,

4
00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:20.165
with 32-bit and 64-bit variants sharing SystemVerilog source.

5
00:00:20.165 --> 00:00:26.915
IP means a reusable hardware design: the core executes instructions, while memory,

6
00:00:26.915 --> 00:00:30.618
peripherals and software complete the system.

7
00:00:30.618 --> 00:00:36.462
We will see embedded control, Linux hosting and accelerator management.

8
00:00:36.462 --> 00:00:43.047
Those uses are possibilities supported by specific configurations and platforms.

9
00:00:43.047 --> 00:00:46.504
They are not guarantees for every variant.

10
00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:53.582
This overview answers three questions: what is CVA6, how does it process instructions,

11
00:00:53.582 --> 00:00:55.969
and where can you learn more?

12
00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:01.484
By the end, you should be able to distinguish a core from a system,

13
00:01:01.484 --> 00:01:08.562
read a configuration example, and identify the evidence needed for a capability claim.

14
00:01:08.562 --> 00:01:12.842
I created this community course for OpenHW Explorer,

15
00:01:12.842 --> 00:01:17.452
interpreting official documentation and project records.

16
00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:23.460
Detailed architecture and verification topics are left for later lessons.

